# Numerics

In python there are two main types of numerics: __<u id='integer'>integers</u>__ and **<u id='float'>Floating point numbers</u>**

## Integers

![](/images/noun-seven-3801475.png){style="width:200px; background:white; border-radius:5px" fig-align="center"}

__<u id='integer'>integers</u>__ are used to store whole numbers.
These should not be declared with quote marks otherwise they will be stored as a <u id='string'>string</u>.

Create the following __markdown cell__:


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#### Integers
```
:::


Create and run the following __code cells__:


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#Roman numeral letters
I = 1
V = 5
X = 10
L = 50
C = 100
D = 500
M = 1000
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
I
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
X
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#7
VII = V + (I * 2)
VII
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#67
LXVII = L + X + VII
LXVII
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#44
XLIV = (L - X) + (V - I)
XLIV
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#2023
MMXXIII = (M * 2) + (X * 2) + (I * 3)
MMXXIII
```
:::


## Floats
![](/images/float.png){style="width:200px; background:white; border-radius:5px; border: white solid 5px" fig-align="center"}

**<u id='float'>Floating point numbers</u>** are used to store real numbers (I.e. a number written with a decimal point dividing the <u id='integer'>integer</u> and the fractional parts).

Create a new __markdown cell__ with the following:


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
#### Floats
```
:::


Run the following code:


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
my_float = 7.0001
another_float = 345.765
yet_another_float = -5.423e+19
```
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
my_float
```
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
another_float
```
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
yet_another_float
```
:::



